{
  "valuation_insights": "### Valuation Insights for Costco Wholesale Corporation (COST)\n\nFor fiscal year 2026 ending August 30, Costco reported total revenue of $303.15 billion [C:revenue], differing from net sales of $297.25 billion as disclosed in the press release [L:10]. The company's reported revenue growth of 10.1% [C:revenue_growth] aligns with total revenue figures, while net sales growth for the fiscal year was also about 10.1% to $297.2 billion as per the release, highlighting the distinction between these two related but different metrics.\n\nOperating income reached $11.685 billion [C:operating_income], yielding an operating margin of 3.85% based on total revenue [C:operating_margin]. This margin, while solid, is below Walmart's 4.18% [C:wmt_operating_margin] and Target's 4.88% [C:tgt_operating_margin], both calculated on comparable total revenue bases.\n\nNet income attributable to Costco was $9.23 billion [C:net_income], with GAAP diluted EPS of $20.76 [C:diluted_eps]. Return on average equity stood at 28.4% [C:return_on_average_equity], reflecting efficient use of shareholder capital.\n\nThe company's debt-to-equity ratio, including current and noncurrent portions of long-term debt, was 0.17 [C:debt_to_equity]. This incorporates $3.91 billion of noncurrent long-term debt plus $2.25 billion of current portion, for a total of $6.16 billion, clarifying that total debt exposure is higher than the previously referenced $3.91 billion (10-K line 85) and reflects conservative leverage.\n\nFree cash flow was $9.38 billion [C:free_cash_flow], providing financial flexibility.\n\nIn the latest quarter, net sales rose 11.2% to $93.9 billion [C:q4_revenue], accompanied by GAAP diluted EPS of $6.75 [C:q4_diluted_eps]. At a recent share price of $904.7, the stock trades at a price-to-earnings ratio of 43.58 [C:price_to_earnings].\n\nCostco’s financials show disciplined debt management amid growing operating income and sustained revenue growth. The operating margin differential versus peers may be linked to Costco's membership-driven pricing strategy, which could compress margin but drive membership value and loyalty.\n\nInsight: Costco's increased current portion of long-term debt to $2.25 billion from $75 million last year, combined with a decrease in noncurrent long-term debt, indicates strategic maturity in debt structuring that optimizes liquidity without raising leverage risk (10-K lines detailing LongTermDebtCurrent and LongTermDebtNoncurrent) [C:debt_to_equity].",
